    "narrative": "Ghost Ride the WHIP enters May 9th with a clear priority on speed and high-ceiling matchups. **Corbin Carroll** (77.94) and **Tyler Soderstrom** (72.72) anchor our production today, with the former providing elite stolen base upside despite facing a relatively stingy battery. **Soderstrom** benefits from a substantial park factor boost, while **Josh Naylor** (67.44) and **Mickey Moniak** (66.69) provide excellent efficiency against their respective right-handed starters. **Moniak** is a specific focal point; despite a moderate SP threat, his platoon advantage and batting order stability make him a high-floor play, even factoring in the risk posed by an elite opposing catcher holding the running game at bay. **James Wood** also earns a prime start at the top of the order, where his xwOBA profile and leadoff positioning maximize his opportunities to manufacture runs.\n\nThe most difficult bench decision involved **Austin Riley**, who logged a respectable 48.69 score but ultimately fell short of our high-efficiency threshold. While his pedigree is unquestioned, the combination of a tough matchup against Snell and the strength of our starting infield depth\u2014namely the surging **Jose Caballero** (60.22)\u2014pushed him to the pine. **Brice Turang** and **Vinnie Pasquantino** remain locked into the starting lineup despite varying degrees of SP resistance, as their situational advantages in the order provide enough categorical weight to justify the start over the likes of **Zach McKinstry** or **Travis Bazzana**. We are monitoring the situation surrounding **Gabriel Moreno**, whose score of 56.85 is anchored by a favorable park and SP matchup, though his low position in the order necessitates caution. Should any lineup shifts occur, particularly for **Alex Freeland**, we are prepared to pivot, but for now, we are prioritizing volume and matchup efficiency to close the gap in SB and AVG.",
